Rotate the image
To rotate the images, we use the wrapAffine() function of the OpenCV library. We need three parameters inside the function—image, rotationMatrix, and dim. These represent the image, its rotation matrix, and its dimensions, respectively.

rotatedImg = cv2.warpAffine(img, rotationMatrix, dim)
Display the translated and rotated image
To display the translated and rotated image, we use the code below:

cv2.imshow("Main",img)
cv2.imshow("TraslatedImg",traslatedImg)
cv2.imshow("RotatedImg",rotatedImg)
The following piece of code explains the overall flow of code and shows the output:

main.py

# Import library
import cv2
import numpy as np

# Loading an image
img = cv2.imread("/usercode/ground.jpg")

# Translating an Image
x = 100
y = -100
translationMatrix = np.float32([[1, 0, x], [0, 1, y]])
dim = (img.shape[1], img.shape[0])
traslatedImg = cv2.warpAffine(img, translationMatrix, dim)

# Rotating an Image
width, height = dim
pointRot = width/2 , height/2
angle = 45
scale = 0.5
rotationMatrix = cv2.getRotationMatrix2D(pointRot, angle, scale)
rotatedImg = cv2.warpAffine(img, rotationMatrix, dim)

# Displaying Image
cv2.imshow("Main", img)
cv2.imshow("TraslatedImg", traslatedImg)
cv2.imshow("RotatedImg", rotatedImg)

cv2.waitKey(0)
